          <Pad Id="6daCLNgDfS7pOf36AJmL9e" Bounds="728,4097,589,46" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="43. Records vs. Classes">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">22</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="2P27aD91B1xtvWsVBGdetk" Name="" Bounds="728,3901,4576,80">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">4</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Pad Id="5IGpBTGCo1zS7ZtEFpqOeY" Bounds="92,2189,540,908" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. MyRecord and MyClass as the working example, not the Particle.** The chapter uses generic placeholder types because the demonstration is purely mechanical — the reader needs to see *just* the mutability behavior, without any application context distracting from it. Chapter 43 will rebuild the Particle as a Class for comparison; chapter 42 keeps things abstract.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. The State Input/Output visual was primed in chapter 39.** Chapter 39 already mentioned that the State Input and State Output are &quot;not visually connected&quot; inside a Record operation, planting the visual cue before naming it. Chapter 42 picks it up — the Class version *is* connected — and the reader recognizes the pattern they have been seeing for chapters. This is deliberate scaffolding.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. Solid vs dashed links also get explicit naming.** Up to this point, the reader has only ever seen solid links. The dashed link is genuinely new visual vocabulary. The chapter names it, explains what it means, and lets the reader see it on the demonstration patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. The chapter does not present Records and Classes as equal alternatives.** The closing column (&quot;What This Means&quot;) explicitly previews that &quot;Class is not free&quot; — the reader leaves chapter 42 knowing that the trade-off discussion is the heart of chapter 43, and that they should not switch to Class casually.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. No decision tree in this chapter.** That's chapter 43's job. Chapter 42 only teaches the mechanic and the visual language.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. No Particle rebuild here either.** That's also chapter 43, where the reader can see the comparison in their own working code.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**7. Length.** Roughly six columns of body text plus two live patches (one inspection element, one full side-by-side demonstration). Comparable in scope to chapter 24 (Update &amp; Create) — a focused mechanic chapter with one central demonstration.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5kPbqyTCwB3W6JfPubterA" Bounds="125,4116,506,198" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The decision chapter. Where the trade-offs get the space they deserve. The reader learns to choose between Record and Class deliberately, with an understanding of what's given up to gain mutability. The Particle from chapter 41 gets rebuilt as a Class for explicit comparison — the reader feels the wiring relief and feels what's been traded for it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is also where the decision tree lives, but as a *derivation* from the mutability mechanic rather than as a memorized list of rules.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5hP1UbY0MvNhbH5jXXItTH" Bounds="125,4328,540,908" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. The decision tree is not a list of rules.** Each section of this chapter is an argument the reader follows — Records are the default because of how VL works; switch to Class for two specific reasons; here is what you give up; here is what it looks like in practice. By the end the reader has derived the decision rather than memorized it. The tree exists implicitly but is not presented as a numbered checklist.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. The &quot;Records as Default&quot; column does heavy lifting.** This is the conceptual foundation of the chapter. Records are not &quot;one of two equal options&quot; — they are the natural fit for VL's functional design. Naming this clearly sets up everything that follows: switching to Class is *deviating from the default*, and the chapter justifies when that deviation makes sense.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. The Particle rebuild is the chapter's centerpiece.** This is where the abstract trade-offs become concrete. The reader sees their own working code from chapter 41 reshaped as a Class and can feel both the wiring relief and the loss of change detection. The pedagogical move is that the reader is not deciding between two abstractions — they are deciding what to do with an application they actually built.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. The &quot;what you give up&quot; column is detailed.** This is the chapter's most important content. The reader needs to know specifically what stops working — Changed, Cache, Snapshots, SampleHold, order of operations. Naming them by name and tying them to the mutability mechanic from chapter 42 makes the cost real instead of vague.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. The &quot;common mistakes&quot; column has only two items.** Deliberately tight. The two mistakes named — switching for wiring relief, switching for unmeasured performance — are the ones a beginner will actually encounter. A longer list would lose the reader; two memorable warnings will stick.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. The collections preview.** A small column that explicitly separates the object-mutability question from the collection-mutability question. This sets up chapter 44 cleanly and prevents the common conflation where &quot;I need performance&quot; becomes &quot;I should use Classes.&quot;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**7. The closing resource is TobyK's full series.** The reader who wants to go deeper has a clear path. The forum thread is also linked because it remains the canonical reference for the question, even though this chapter has updated and reframed its answer.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**8. Length.** Roughly six columns of body text plus two substantial live patches (the Changed demonstration and the Particle rebuild). Comparable in scope to chapter 42 — these two chapters are a natural pair.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**9. What this chapter does *not* do.** It does not introduce mutable collections (chapter 44). It does not introduce Process-enabled types or Dispose (chapter 45). It stays squarely on the Record-vs-Class decision and lets the reader walk away with that question settled.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1XxlNlve6TdR2G3OKIS1NY" Bounds="2088,2385,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=820):**&#xD;&#xA;- A small inspection patch showing the SetColor operation for MyRecord and MyClass side-by-side, opened in nested patch views or referenced via Patch Explorer.&#xD;&#xA;- Annotation IOBoxes pointing at the State Input and State Output pins on each, noting the visual difference (not-connected vs connected).">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5jSEAYFgRdZ3JkEdIYW7yw" Bounds="2536,2518,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1180, width ~1400):**&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The MyRecord/MyClass side-by-side patch as we discussed earlier in the conversation:&#xD;&#xA;- Left half: `Create MyRecord` → `SetColor (blue)` → `SetColor (red)` chain. Three `Color MyRecord` reader nodes tapped in at start, middle, end. They display white, blue, red.&#xD;&#xA;- Right half: `Create MyClass` → `SetColor (blue)` → `SetColor (red)` chain. Three `Color MyClass` reader nodes tapped in at start, middle, end. They display red, red, red.&#xD;&#xA;- Links on the left are solid; links on the right are dashed.&#xD;&#xA;- An annotation pointing at the middle readers (&quot;this is where they disagree&quot;) to draw the eye to the key moment.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1jP2lNHaYAlCeMyB2MJmU6" Bounds="4034,2153,358,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Mutability in vvvv Gamma (Record vs Class) by TobyK">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="363rMLwR0fP7qAy8DsVxy5" Bounds="4034,2182,360,54" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://youtu.be/zGufG64WSF4">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Link</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1MiEQYrVqG1sHkx8VaHOiu" Bounds="728,4187,401,459" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The last chapter showed the mechanic. This chapter helps you choose. When should you reach for a Class instead of a Record? The honest answer is — less often than you might think. Records are the default for almost everything you will build in VL, and the cases where Classes are the right move are specific and worth recognizing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;We will look at the genuine reasons to choose a Class, the real costs of that choice, and how it plays out in a concrete case — the Particle from chapter 41, rebuilt as a Class so you can compare them side by side. By the end of this chapter you will be able to make the call confidently, instead of switching based on a hunch.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4rUakWEQhbtc6t7MM0Qy62" Bounds="1196,4201,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Records as Default">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4yKJ8nS3y1MmCkuGpeODFF" Bounds="1196,4243,405,467" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="VL is a functional environment at its core. Values flow through your patch from one operation to the next, each step producing a new result, and the language is designed around that flow. Records fit this design naturally. They are values. They behave the way numbers, strings, and built-in types behave — passed through operations, transformed, copied along the way.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;This is why Record is the default choice for almost any custom data type. The functional model is not just a style — it is how the rest of the language works. Change detection assumes values flow through the patch and that new ones differ from old ones. Caching assumes the same. Snapshots and history rely on the fact that each value is preserved at the moment it existed.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;When you use a Record, you get all of this automatically. You can detect when the Record changed. You can cache results. You can hold a snapshot and compare it later. None of this requires extra work — it is what the language does when you let values flow through it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The starting question is therefore not &quot;should I use a Class?&quot; The starting question is &quot;do I have a specific reason not to use a Record?&quot; If you cannot point at one, you almost certainly want a Record.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3eAIIdzRZwkDEA2ReSg40e" Bounds="1650,4181,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="When to Switch to Class">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2LdDnyRYI8366Luj68D4Pf" Bounds="1650,4223,405,548" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="There are two reasons that are worth recognizing as a beginner.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The first is access from multiple locations. Suppose you have an object that needs to be modified from several different places in your patch — one part of the application changes its Position, another changes its Color, a third changes its State. With a Record, each modification has to read the current Record from its pad, apply the change, and store it back. If three places are doing this in the same frame, you have to be careful about which one runs first, and the wiring gets dense. With a Class, each location simply calls the appropriate operation directly. The Class modifies itself, and every part of the patch sees the change immediately. The wiring disappears.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The second is performance. A Record copies itself on every modification. For a single Record this cost is invisible. For a thousand Records all updating every frame, the cost can add up — a thousand new copies created and old copies discarded each frame. A Class has no such cost; it modifies itself in place. If you have measured a performance problem and traced it to object copying, switching to a Class is one tool that can help.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Notice the qualifier on the second reason — if you have measured. Switching to Classes for performance without measuring first is one of the most common mistakes. The cost of Record copying is usually invisible, and the cost of giving up Records' features for an imagined performance gain is real.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="7CRKbPJppAWsY6NQnXehtn" Bounds="2098,4179,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="What You Give Up">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="78f7Qq7IKeZjpoi4hxNXIx" Bounds="2098,4221,404,619" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The relief of not wiring back to a pad comes at a price. The features that Records give you automatically do not work the same way with Classes.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The most visible loss is change detection. The Changed node and Cache regions watch a link for changes. With a Record link, every modification produces a new value, and Changed fires. With a Class link, the link carries the same reference even when the underlying object is modified, and Changed does not fire. The Gray Book is honest about this — Changed could really be called ChangedReference. It detects when a new Class instance arrives, not when the existing one is modified.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Snapshots and Undo work similarly. With Records, holding a snapshot of a value means holding that value — the Record will not change because it cannot. With Classes, holding a &quot;snapshot&quot; means holding a reference, and the referenced Class can be modified between when you took the snapshot and when you tried to use it. SampleHold has the same issue.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Order of operations becomes less obvious too. With Records, modifications happen serially because you explicitly wire them. With Classes, two parts of the patch can both modify the same Class instance in the same frame, and the order is sometimes determined by the F9 execution order rather than anything visible in the patch. The wiring relief that drew you to Classes is also the loss of clarity about when each modification happens.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You can work around all of these — there are patterns using BehaviourSubject, ticket counters, and explicit Clone operations. But the workarounds are advanced material, and they exist to recover what Records give you for free.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2KMjgtHZSotpx5j4lZ2KjQ" Bounds="2101,4858,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=920):**&#xD;&#xA;A small dry demonstration of Changed not firing:&#xD;&#xA;- A Record in a pad, with SetColor called on it via a button click. A Changed node downstream fires each time the button is clicked.&#xD;&#xA;- A Class in a pad, with SetColor called on it via a button click. A Changed node downstream does not fire — the reference stays the same even though the Color is now different.&#xD;&#xA;- IOBoxes labeled to make the contrast unmissable.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5yYWu8MwWZHL6DdOFPNYsx" Bounds="834,4740,355,127" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="List and specify more intermediate reasons why to switch to a class! But only demonstrate the pad-less thingie.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1X5QEEJlugmyZgQyENKR2B" Bounds="2568,4182,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The Particle, Rebuilt">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5U4mCE8PMhBxFHmrouELLC" Bounds="2568,4224,404,557" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Now let's look at this on the application from chapter 41. The Particle was a Record. We stored a Spread&lt;Particle&gt; in a pad. Each frame we read the spread, called Update on each Particle, and stored the spread back into the pad. The explicit-store pattern was everywhere.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The patch below shows the same application rebuilt with Particle as a Class. The structure is almost the same — a Spread&lt;Particle&gt;, a ForEach, calls to Update on each Particle. But the storing-back pattern is gone. Update modifies each Class in place. The spread still has to be in a pad so it persists across frames, but the individual Particles do not need to be stored back into the spread — they are the same Particle each frame, just with new Position.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Compare the two patches. The Class version is shorter. The data flow is less complicated. The wiring relief is real.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;But look at what is no longer there. With the Record version, you could put a Changed node on the spread and detect when the application state changed in any way. With the Class version, that no longer works — the spread is the same spread even when its Particles' positions are all different. If you wanted change-driven logic in your application, the Record version supported it. The Class version requires you to add explicit notification.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Most beginner applications never use this feature, and the wiring relief feels like a clear win. Most intermediate applications do use it, sometimes without realizing. The trade is worth making with eyes open.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6sKTDSI6JgZPjxny63mSxT" Bounds="2571,4814,401,228" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1200, width ~1400):**&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Side-by-side patches:&#xD;&#xA;- **Left half:** Particle-as-Record from chapter 41 (or simplified version of it) — pad with Spread&lt;Particle&gt;, ForEach with Update, store back to pad.&#xD;&#xA;- **Right half:** Same application, with Particle now a Class. ForEach with Update but no store-back at the Particle level. The spread pad is still there but the inner wiring is reduced.&#xD;&#xA;- Both produce identical visual output on their respective SkiaRenderers.&#xD;&#xA;- Annotations pointing at the wiring difference, and another set pointing at where Changed-style detection would or would not work.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6lsg68W20c7wUvW7Hackb6" Bounds="3053,4178,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Common Mistakes">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2HyzUc46N3NTfBsHOr8bLS" Bounds="3053,4220,404,321" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Two patterns to watch for, both common when patchers first discover Classes.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Reaching for a Class because the Record wiring feels verbose. The wiring is not noise — it is the patch making the data flow explicit. Verbosity that you can read is more valuable than terseness that hides behavior. Switch to a Class when you have a specific reason from the previous columns, not because the Record version &quot;feels&quot; heavier than it should.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Reaching for a Class for performance without measuring. The cost of Record copying is usually negligible. If you are unsure whether you have a performance problem, you do not have one. If you do have one, measure first — find out where the cost actually is. The answer is often a different patch structure or a mutable collection (chapter 44), not a switch from Record to Class.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4e0vW95AuV41wAjNBBOwfv" Bounds="3507,4175,412,34" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Collections Are a Separate Question">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="7jxSraUj0SFpXP9dMOubmu" Bounds="3507,4217,404,321" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="One last thing worth naming before the next chapter. The choice between Record and Class is about individual objects. There is a separate choice about collections — whether to use Spread (immutable) or SpreadBuilder (mutable) for the container that holds many of them. These two questions are independent.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You can have an immutable Spread of mutable Classes. You can have a mutable SpreadBuilder of immutable Records. Both are sensible patterns for different reasons. Chapter 44 looks at the collection question on its own terms — when to use mutable collection types, what they cost, and how they relate to object mutability.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;For now, just notice that &quot;I need performance&quot; rarely means &quot;I should switch from Records to Classes.&quot; It often means &quot;I should use a mutable collection.&quot; The distinction matters.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="0vlMhTEGbyYjd8xnMacAdf" Bounds="4123,4163,383,298">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">10</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
